WEBVTT

1
00:44:15.560 --> 00:44:17.880
So hello everyone again.

2
00:44:17.880 --> 00:44:21.040
I am Mehulkumar Desai
and I am a research scholar at Indian

3
00:44:21.040 --> 00:44:24.880
Institute of Technology,
Indian School of Mines, Denmark.

4
00:44:25.240 --> 00:44:27.520
My research work is situated
at the intersection

5
00:44:27.520 --> 00:44:29.560
of electronic literature,
interactive digital

6
00:44:29.560 --> 00:44:33.320
narratives, digital humanities,
and creative industries.

7
00:44:33.440 --> 00:44:38.600
I am also an affiliated researcher
at Electronic Literature Lab,

8
00:44:38.640 --> 00:44:41.600
Washington State University, Vancouver.

9
00:44:41.600 --> 00:44:46.200
So for my present study,

10
00:44:46.360 --> 00:44:50.320
Doctor Shanmugapriya,
who is my supervisor,

11
00:44:50.320 --> 00:44:55.040
and I have been spending the last one year
choosing one question

12
00:44:55.400 --> 00:44:58.400
that started almost by accident.

13
00:44:58.680 --> 00:45:02.640
Why does India have 420 million video

14
00:45:02.680 --> 00:45:05.760
game players
and a thriving digital art scene,

15
00:45:05.960 --> 00:45:09.200
but almost no one talking about
Indian electronic literature,

16
00:45:09.200 --> 00:45:12.200
and people are still studying it as a—

17
00:45:12.280 --> 00:45:15.360
considering it as a nascent field.

18
00:45:15.840 --> 00:45:19.160
So today
we will walk you through what we found

19
00:45:19.160 --> 00:45:21.800
and what we think we should do about it.

20
00:45:28.320 --> 00:45:28.760
We will

21
00:45:28.760 --> 00:45:32.760
move to five stops: the paradox itself.

22
00:45:32.960 --> 00:45:35.960
The theoretical lens
we borrow to study it.

23
00:45:35.960 --> 00:45:39.360
Two success stories
for video games and digital art.

24
00:45:39.520 --> 00:45:41.640
A harder look at why electronic literature

25
00:45:41.640 --> 00:45:44.880
hasn't kept pace,
ecause the digital divide

26
00:45:45.640 --> 00:45:50.160
turns out to be necessary,
but not sufficient as an explanation.

27
00:45:50.360 --> 00:45:54.200
And finally,
what we propose to do about it.

28
00:45:56.440 --> 00:45:58.520
Here is the puzzle in numbers.

29
00:45:58.520 --> 00:46:01.600
India's video game market made $868

30
00:46:01.600 --> 00:46:06.800
million in 2023,
up almost 16% year on year,

31
00:46:06.800 --> 00:46:11.040
and it's projected to reach
1.6 billion by 2027.

32
00:46:11.320 --> 00:46:16.160
India now has over 420 million online
gamers, second only to China.

33
00:46:16.320 --> 00:46:20.000
Digital art doesn't yet
have its own dedicated revenue figure,

34
00:46:20.080 --> 00:46:25.040
so as a proxy, scholars
point to Indian art auction market $144

35
00:46:25.040 --> 00:46:30.080
million in financial year
2023, nearly half of it now

36
00:46:31.600 --> 00:46:33.120
transacted online.

37
00:46:33.120 --> 00:46:37.120
That's the sales channel,
not the art form itself.

38
00:46:37.120 --> 00:46:39.240
And electronic literature.

39
00:46:39.240 --> 00:46:42.240
Three waves of practice since 2004.

40
00:46:42.640 --> 00:46:44.000
As highlighted by Dr.

41
00:46:44.000 --> 00:46:47.000
Shanmugapriya,
four Indian works finally made

42
00:46:47.000 --> 00:46:50.120
into the Electronic Literature
Collection, volume four,

43
00:46:50.240 --> 00:46:54.320
and by our own reading of the policy
landscape, it is simply absent

44
00:46:54.320 --> 00:46:57.320
from any national creative industry
framework.

45
00:46:57.560 --> 00:46:59.320
Same computational origins.

46
00:46:59.320 --> 00:47:03.560
Are there still, say,
completely different facts appeared

47
00:47:03.680 --> 00:47:06.680
when it comes to creative industries?

48
00:47:07.360 --> 00:47:12.080
That gap is what drew up the study
and we built it around three questions.

49
00:47:12.120 --> 00:47:14.280
Why video games in digital
art more visible

50
00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:17.840
than electronic literature
in Indian digital creative culture.?

51
00:47:18.680 --> 00:47:22.920
Second, what technological, economic,
and cultural factors explain that?

52
00:47:23.560 --> 00:47:26.200
And third, the one we care

53
00:47:26.200 --> 00:47:31.640
most about, what strategies could actually
help electronic literature earn

54
00:47:31.680 --> 00:47:37.000
the engagement it deserves among readers
and players and inside academia itself?

55
00:47:38.800 --> 00:47:41.560
So let me begin with the global context.

56
00:47:41.560 --> 00:47:44.560
A bit of global scale. First,

57
00:47:44.840 --> 00:47:46.840
creative economy outlook

58
00:47:46.840 --> 00:47:51.560
put the global creative economy
at $2.3 trillion

59
00:47:51.600 --> 00:47:58.200
a year, 3.1% of world’s
GDP, 6.2% of world's employment.

60
00:47:58.200 --> 00:48:02.080
But there is no single agreed definition
of creative industries still there.

61
00:48:02.120 --> 00:48:05.600
Five major models have been developed
from the UK's

62
00:48:05.600 --> 00:48:10.960
1998 DCMS model, which ties creativity
to intellectual property

63
00:48:11.000 --> 00:48:14.440
to [?] industry framework.

64
00:48:14.520 --> 00:48:17.560
For this study,
we lean on one model in particular

65
00:48:17.600 --> 00:48:21.480
because it gives us
the most useful names for India,

66
00:48:22.440 --> 00:48:26.680
which is Throsby’s 
concentric circle model.

67
00:48:27.360 --> 00:48:30.760
At the center sits
the purest form of artistic creativity:

68
00:48:30.800 --> 00:48:34.160
literature, music,
visual and performing arts.

69
00:48:34.200 --> 00:48:38.560
Moving outward, culture, content,
themes, and commercial content thickens.

70
00:48:38.760 --> 00:48:42.760
Third, broadcasting, publishing and sound
recording next,

71
00:48:42.800 --> 00:48:46.640
then fashion design and architecture,
and finally advertising

72
00:48:46.640 --> 00:48:49.640
software and cultural tourism at the outer
edge.

73
00:48:49.840 --> 00:48:53.280
Throsby’s rule: the more cultural content
or good

74
00:48:53.280 --> 00:48:57.200
carries, the stronger its claim
to sit inside the creative industries.

75
00:48:57.320 --> 00:49:00.840
Later in this talk, we are going to
redraw this model for India.

76
00:49:02.160 --> 00:49:02.600
India has

77
00:49:02.600 --> 00:49:06.480
spent the last decade building real policy
infrastructure for digital creativity.

78
00:49:06.520 --> 00:49:10.960
One of the states, Karnataka,
passed the country's first AGCP,

79
00:49:11.040 --> 00:49:15.160
which stands for Audiovisual Graphics
and Comics Policy in 2012.

80
00:49:15.600 --> 00:49:20.040
Reliance news 2016
launch collapsed the data post overnight.

81
00:49:20.320 --> 00:49:23.720
In 2022,
the central government stood up and

82
00:49:24.400 --> 00:49:29.360
and AGCP task force was formed.

83
00:49:29.680 --> 00:49:33.200
Karnataka opened India's
first center of excellence and Telangana

84
00:49:33.240 --> 00:49:36.960
launched its own image initiative by 2024

85
00:49:37.000 --> 00:49:40.800
that became the Indian Institute
of Creative Technology.

86
00:49:40.800 --> 00:49:44.840
And 2025 Waves Summit launched

87
00:49:45.040 --> 00:49:48.760
a Create in India Challenge
spanning 34 categories.

88
00:49:49.440 --> 00:49:52.080
Every rung of that ladder, electronic

89
00:49:52.080 --> 00:49:55.080
literature is still missing.

90
00:49:56.400 --> 00:49:58.520
So first case study: video game.

91
00:49:58.520 --> 00:50:01.960
So what does support translating to

92
00:50:03.160 --> 00:50:06.200
$868 million in 2023,

93
00:50:06.240 --> 00:50:09.240
heading towards 1.6 billion by 2027?

94
00:50:09.240 --> 00:50:12.520
420 million gamers, second to China.

95
00:50:12.560 --> 00:50:18.000
78% of that revenue
already came from mobile back in 2023,

96
00:50:18.040 --> 00:50:22.200
148 million people now pay to play,

97
00:50:22.240 --> 00:50:26.400
spending an average of $22 a year each.

98
00:50:26.480 --> 00:50:29.480
And the studio count tells its own story.

99
00:50:29.480 --> 00:50:32.360
25 game studios in 2010,

100
00:50:32.360 --> 00:50:35.560
275 by 2019.

101
00:50:36.520 --> 00:50:39.040
But the numbers alone don't explain why.

102
00:50:39.040 --> 00:50:41.760
Four things why?

103
00:50:41.760 --> 00:50:45.560
Whereas the four things that help
video games grow in India.

104
00:50:45.600 --> 00:50:47.640
First, and this is the paradox.

105
00:50:47.640 --> 00:50:53.560
Within paradox, gray markets pirated
copies and cloned consoles at large.

106
00:50:53.560 --> 00:50:59.240
Lajpat Rai Market and Nehru Place
at Delhi taught a generation

107
00:50:59.240 --> 00:51:04.280
how to play with vendors
doubling as technicians and teachers.

108
00:51:04.560 --> 00:51:07.760
Second is June 2016 launch, obviously.

109
00:51:07.800 --> 00:51:12.720
Third is UPI, which crossed 20 billion
monthly transactions

110
00:51:12.800 --> 00:51:16.520
worth nearly 25 lakh rupees by August

111
00:51:16.520 --> 00:51:21.240
2025,
making microtransactions frictionless

112
00:51:21.680 --> 00:51:26.560
and for cultural confidence, games
like Raji and ancient epic turned

113
00:51:26.600 --> 00:51:32.000
mandala motifs and mythology into globally
exportable intellectual property.

114
00:51:32.920 --> 00:51:36.080
Moving on to our second case study,
digital art.

115
00:51:36.760 --> 00:51:40.120
Digital art tells a quieter
but equally important story.

116
00:51:40.160 --> 00:51:43.760
It starts in 1999 with Baiju

117
00:51:45.080 --> 00:51:48.080
translating painting into interactive HTML

118
00:51:48.080 --> 00:51:50.880
through the 2000s
and the 2010s, institutions

119
00:51:50.880 --> 00:51:54.680
like Code Studio,
The Cyber Mohalla and Symbol,

120
00:51:54.880 --> 00:51:58.720
and archival projects like Padma
give the field real infrastructure.

121
00:51:58.760 --> 00:52:01.720
Today, artists like to use AI

122
00:52:01.720 --> 00:52:05.080
as a medium of what he calls
aesthetic decolonialism,

123
00:52:05.240 --> 00:52:11.160
and as the British Council's own 2024
report puts it— I am putting it here.

124
00:52:11.200 --> 00:52:15.800
India now has a rich, growing ecosystem
of artists and creative practitioners

125
00:52:15.800 --> 00:52:18.800
working at the intersection
of art and technology.

126
00:52:20.600 --> 00:52:23.680
So moving on to the print literature.

127
00:52:23.720 --> 00:52:26.360
So where does print literature itself
fit in?

128
00:52:26.360 --> 00:52:30.560
Print publishing already
made this transition, whilst book market

129
00:52:30.560 --> 00:52:34.400
was worth $10.4 billion in 2024,

130
00:52:34.440 --> 00:52:37.840
heading towards 14.6 billion by 2030.

131
00:52:37.920 --> 00:52:43.560
That's because back in 1998, the UK's DCMS
mapping document reframed publishing

132
00:52:43.560 --> 00:52:48.360
as an intellectual property industry
and economic sector, not just an art form.

133
00:52:48.400 --> 00:52:50.520
Print made that leap.

134
00:52:50.520 --> 00:52:54.680
Whereas born-digital descendants such
as electronic literature still hasn't.

135
00:52:54.760 --> 00:52:58.760
That unfinished migration
is really the heart of what comes next.

136
00:53:00.480 --> 00:53:02.120
In India,

137
00:53:02.120 --> 00:53:05.720
Indian electronic literature
has actually moved through three waves.

138
00:53:06.760 --> 00:53:09.840
Wave one started in 2004,
as highlighted by Dr.

139
00:53:09.840 --> 00:53:12.960
Shanmugapriya’s
study with the SMS novel clock,

140
00:53:13.480 --> 00:53:16.560
a genuinely different
starting point from the Western canon.

141
00:53:17.040 --> 00:53:19.680
Wave second, roughly between 2009

142
00:53:19.680 --> 00:53:23.840
to 2019, when her research published,

143
00:53:24.280 --> 00:53:28.080
writes the rise of social media
and we are proposing the third wave.

144
00:53:28.240 --> 00:53:33.240
Where we are now has brought twin based
hypertext digital theater performance

145
00:53:33.760 --> 00:53:38.160
during the pandemic
and real international recognition

146
00:53:38.280 --> 00:53:41.280
for Indian works
in Indian electronic literature.

147
00:53:42.680 --> 00:53:45.720
Four works have made up to electronic
literature collections volume

148
00:53:45.720 --> 00:53:50.120
four and Indian Electronic Literature
Anthology one and second is there.

149
00:53:51.360 --> 00:53:56.360
So if we do— if we put these three
industry side-by-side, the contrast is

150
00:53:56.360 --> 00:53:56.720
stark.

151
00:53:56.720 --> 00:54:00.240
Video games have a dedicated task force
and a national center of excellence.

152
00:54:00.280 --> 00:54:01.880
Digital art has real recognition

153
00:54:01.880 --> 00:54:06.240
through mapping exercises
like treaties, councils, and UKRI study.

154
00:54:06.280 --> 00:54:07.800
Electronic literature has neither.

155
00:54:07.800 --> 00:54:11.760
Absent in our reading from every national
creative industry framework.

156
00:54:11.800 --> 00:54:14.480
Video games
draw venture capital and publisher deals.

157
00:54:14.480 --> 00:54:18.360
Digital art leaves on
grants, NFT sales, gallery patronages.

158
00:54:18.400 --> 00:54:22.160
Whereas electronic literature survives
mostly on personal interests.

159
00:54:22.440 --> 00:54:24.720
Games reach 420 million players.

160
00:54:24.720 --> 00:54:28.040
Electronic literary cases
are small, largely academic readership.

161
00:54:30.120 --> 00:54:30.640
Here is

162
00:54:30.640 --> 00:54:33.840
where we push back a little on existing
scholarship.

163
00:54:33.880 --> 00:54:37.960
Everyone rightly points
to the digital divide, but we think that

164
00:54:39.800 --> 00:54:43.120
digital divide
really exists through three layers.

165
00:54:43.160 --> 00:54:44.400
The first is access.

166
00:54:44.400 --> 00:54:48.040
Nearly half of India still lacks
reliable, affordable Internet.

167
00:54:48.080 --> 00:54:49.320
The second is literacy.

168
00:54:49.320 --> 00:54:52.840
Many creators lack the technical skills
to build for this medium.

169
00:54:52.880 --> 00:54:55.880
The third, this is the one people miss,

170
00:54:55.880 --> 00:55:00.200
is an outcome divide everywhere,
even when access exists.

171
00:55:00.240 --> 00:55:01.240
Audience often lack

172
00:55:01.240 --> 00:55:04.600
the digital literacy to actually read
and appreciate electronic literature,

173
00:55:05.280 --> 00:55:09.920
and they would consider it
similar to YouTube Shorts

174
00:55:09.920 --> 00:55:13.680
or any entertainment industry,
and print centric academic

175
00:55:13.680 --> 00:55:17.040
bias and algorithmic
invisibility are still there.

176
00:55:17.400 --> 00:55:20.400
If you try to search
electronic literature in Google,

177
00:55:20.640 --> 00:55:24.200
you will not be able to reach
to a substantial results.

178
00:55:25.640 --> 00:55:26.120
So we

179
00:55:26.120 --> 00:55:30.280
modified Throsby’s concept model
for India.

180
00:55:30.280 --> 00:55:32.640
Instead of treating electronic literature
as marginal,

181
00:55:32.640 --> 00:55:35.720
we place it back at the very center
alongside literature, music,

182
00:55:35.720 --> 00:55:39.480
and the performance arts
as equally central symbolic production.

183
00:55:39.480 --> 00:55:42.080
Video games and digital
art moved to the wider cultural ring

184
00:55:42.080 --> 00:55:45.000
where hybrid, symbolic,
and commercial practice leaves.

185
00:55:45.000 --> 00:55:47.120
This isn't just a diagram, it's a claim.

186
00:55:47.120 --> 00:55:51.040
Electronic literature's marginal status
is not a verdict on its cultural,

187
00:55:51.040 --> 00:55:55.160
but its policy failure,
and policy failures can be fixed.

188
00:55:56.000 --> 00:55:58.200
So that leads to the culmination

189
00:55:58.200 --> 00:56:03.560
of— a foundation of Indian Consortium
for Interactive Digital Narratives, ICIDN,

190
00:56:03.800 --> 00:56:08.680
which we co-founded
to solve these problems.

191
00:56:08.920 --> 00:56:09.800
Four things.

192
00:56:09.800 --> 00:56:13.600
Model directly on what worked for games
and digital art, build skills

193
00:56:13.600 --> 00:56:16.840
through workshops
and lecture series, preserve and showcase

194
00:56:16.840 --> 00:56:20.280
through a living digital archive,
and open calls for new works

195
00:56:20.880 --> 00:56:24.000
creators through the Indian New Media
Writing Prize.

196
00:56:24.160 --> 00:56:29.200
We recently could manage to add
an Indian category in New Media

197
00:56:29.200 --> 00:56:33.840
Writing prize and sustain the fields
financially through distribution pathways,

198
00:56:33.840 --> 00:56:37.680
such as a subscription platform
that pays creators directly

199
00:56:37.680 --> 00:56:41.920
the same economic logic that already works
for OTP platforms and NFTs.

200
00:56:42.080 --> 00:56:44.760
So we will be building a distribution
pathways

201
00:56:44.760 --> 00:56:46.760
for electronic literature in India.

202
00:56:46.760 --> 00:56:48.680
So to close electronic

203
00:56:48.680 --> 00:56:52.320
literature's marginal status
in India is structural, not inherent.

204
00:56:52.400 --> 00:56:56.880
It reflects exclusion from policy,
not a lack of creative potential.

205
00:56:57.040 --> 00:57:00.280
Video games and digital art prove Indian
audience and institutions

206
00:57:00.320 --> 00:57:03.520
we invest when the infrastructure exist.

207
00:57:03.840 --> 00:57:06.200
This is an ongoing project.

208
00:57:06.200 --> 00:57:10.240
ICIDN is our attempt
to build such infrastructures.

209
00:57:10.240 --> 00:57:14.560
It is also part of my doctoral research
which focuses on

210
00:57:14.560 --> 00:57:19.120
creating infrastructures for the creation,
dissemination, and preservation

211
00:57:19.160 --> 00:57:22.800
of interactive digital narratives
and electronic literature in India.

212
00:57:22.880 --> 00:57:26.160
If it proved successful here,
we believe the model can be adapted

213
00:57:26.160 --> 00:57:30.240
to support similar ecosystems
across the Global South.

214
00:57:30.640 --> 00:57:31.800
Thank you.

215
00:57:31.800 --> 00:57:34.480
These are the references. Thank you.
